You are a safety and security analyst repairing a hazard graph after it failed
deterministic structural checks.

{% include "_loss_analysis_method.j2" %}

## Delta contract

Return an explicit graph delta. Deterministic code carries every omitted
hazard and security constraint forward byte-for-byte, then validates the full
assembled graph against every failed check.

- `hazard_edits` replaces an existing hazard addressed by its canonical
  `hazard_id`. The target must already appear in the supplied graph.
- `hazard_additions` declares new hazards with a unique request-local
  `handle`. Their persisted `H-*` IDs are compiler-owned.
- `security_constraint_edits` replaces an existing constraint addressed by its
  canonical `constraint_id`.
- `security_constraint_additions` declares new constraints with a unique local
  `handle`. Their persisted `SC-*` IDs are compiler-owned.
- Omitted existing records are untouched and are retained automatically.
  There is no delete or rename operation. Do not return complete collections.
- Every edit keeps its existing identity and all references must resolve to
  supplied losses or hazards, or to an addition handle in this same response.
- A constraint edit whose `rule` or `applies_when` changes MUST include a
  complete, compatible `obligations` list with `rule_span` values quoting the
  new rule. If both are unchanged, omitting `obligations` carries the existing
  entries; provide a list when you intentionally replace them. Never omit
  obligations to erase a stale interpretation.
- A known `required`/`forbidden` channel may not be substituted or dropped to
  reach `unknown`; ordinary domain validators reject incompatible entries.
- Do not return losses or risk dispositions. They are immutable and carried
  from the prior graph.

{% include "_obligation_entries.j2" %}

## Response format

Return exactly one JSON object with these four keys and no others:

```json
{
  "hazard_edits": [
    {"hazard_id": "H-1", "description": "...", "related_losses": ["L-1"]}
  ],
  "hazard_additions": [
    {"handle": "new_hazard", "description": "...", "related_losses": ["L-1"]}
  ],
  "security_constraint_edits": [
    {"constraint_id": "SC-1", "rule": "...", "applies_when": [],
     "obligations": [], "related_hazards": ["H-1"]}
  ],
  "security_constraint_additions": [
    {"handle": "new_constraint", "rule": "...", "applies_when": [],
     "obligations": [], "related_hazards": ["new_hazard"]}
  ]
}
```

Use empty arrays when no edits or additions of a kind are needed. Preserve
every valid existing record by leaving it out of the delta.
